Overlay patch 0047: the Sense pair becomes a pad (VR-DONOR-MAP 8,
`padmgr.c`). The donor's six-input taxonomy -- TRIGGER, GRIP, PRIMARY,
SECONDARY, THUMBCLICK, MENU per hand -- is OR'd into the N64 pad so the hands
can play the game, while the gamepad stays the shipped default for everything
the hands do not cover.

WHERE. Inside PadMgr_HandleRetraceMsg, IMMEDIATELY AFTER
`osContGetReadData(padMgr->pads)` and BEFORE `PadMgr_ProcessInputs`, which is
the donor's own hook site and the only correct one: ProcessInputs is what
derives this tick's press/rel edges from cur-vs-prev. Hooking after it would
mean synthesising every edge by hand; hooking before it means a Sense button
gets exactly the same edge treatment as a physical one, for free.

WHAT. Everything is OR'd into `padMgr->pads[0].button` -- nothing is cleared --
so a keyboard or a connected gamepad keeps working alongside the hands. The
left Sense stick drives the N64 analog stick ONLY while it is actually
deflected (|axis| > 0.02); writing it unconditionally would zero a gamepad's
stick every tick whenever a Sense pair happened to be paired.

THE PROFILES. Two, and as of rev4 (VR R9 part B) they are built from ROLES
rather than written per physical hand -- because the sword hand is a shipped
setting (R9 part A) and a table written as "left" and "right" silently stops
being the user's table the moment he switches it.

rev4 applies the user's 1.0.1.12 ruling on top of rev3:

  "Right stick click: unbind (no C-UP, it's useless in VR mode). Left face
   buttons mirror the right: bottom = A, top = B. Keep START on left stick click
   and both MENU buttons."

  CLASSIC, the shipped binding. ROLE columns first (they mirror on
  gSohVRLeftHanded), then the physical ones (they do not, because the STICKS do
  not: the left unit is always the movement stick and the right is always the
  turn):
    OFF hand   TRIGGER = Z (always, under every condition)
    SWORD hand TRIGGER = B (attack; also the mirrored item trigger)
    OFF hand   GRIP    = R (hold shield, and the crouch)
    SWORD hand GRIP    = -- (the item wheel; no N64 bit at all)
    both       PRIMARY (bottom face) = A      <- rev4: symmetric
    both       SECONDARY (top face)  = B      <- rev4: symmetric
    LEFT unit  THUMBCLICK = START             <- rev4: kept
    RIGHT unit THUMBCLICK = --                <- rev4: UNBOUND (was C-UP)
    both       MENU = START

  rev3 had left PRIMARY=START / SECONDARY=A and right THUMBCLICK=C-UP. the user
  wore that and asked for the face buttons to read the same on both hands, and
  for the right thumbclick to do nothing. First person is still reachable: it is
  a C-UP, and C-UP is on the item wheel like every other C item.

  OCARINA, which OVERRIDES classic while the ocarina is ACTUALLY OUT, also by
  role --
    OFF   : TRIGGER=C-down GRIP=Z  PRIMARY=C-left  SECONDARY=C-up  MENU=START
    SWORD : TRIGGER=A      GRIP=R  PRIMARY=C-right SECONDARY=B     MENU=START
  i.e. the five notes are D4=A (sword trigger), F4=C-down (off trigger),
  A4=C-right (sword bottom face), B4=C-left (off bottom face), D5=C-up (off
  top face); sharpen=R on the sword grip, flatten=Z on the off grip,
  put-away=B on the sword top face. Every note is on a trigger or a face button,
  which is the whole reason the profile exists.

  AND, rev4: THE RIGHT STICK BECOMES THE C-PAD -- HERE AND ONLY HERE. the user:
  "It should ONLY trigger C buttons when you're holding ocarina." That is now
  literally true: overlay 0052 takes the Sense pair out of SDL's gamepad
  enumeration entirely, so no stick of theirs can raise a C bit through LUS's
  axis mapping in any mode; and the ONE place a stick may become a C button is
  this block, while the ocarina is genuinely out. Deflection past 25% on the
  RIGHT unit's stick sets C-left/right/up/down as raw STATE, which is what
  ocarina notes need -- PadMgr_ProcessInputs derives the press and release edges
  from it a few lines later, exactly as it does for a physical button.

THE OCARINA PREDICATE, AND THE BUG THAT WAS IN IT (rev3, VR R8 part B).

  rev1/rev2 used `gPlayState->msgCtx.ocarinaMode != 0`. THAT FIELD IS A LATCH,
  not a "the ocarina is out" flag. z_message_PAL.c sets OCARINA_MODE_04 when the
  player puts the ocarina away and the player actor CONSUMES it
  (z_player.c:14958) without clearing it; the only writers of OCARINA_MODE_00
  are Message_Init, the textbox openers, and a handful of actors. So after ANY
  ocarina use the field stays non-zero until the next textbox opens -- often for
  the rest of a play session.

  That is exactly what the user reported: "bottom face button on right controller
  is C RIGHT. left trigger is C DOWN. WHy?!" Both are the OCARINA row, verbatim,
  and the ocarina profile had simply never been switched back off. Overlay 0039
  used the same predicate to suppress the VR turn, so the smooth turn died the
  same way and for the same reason.

  The honest predicate is the message system's MODE, which is what "the ocarina
  is out" actually means: msgMode in [MSGMODE_OCARINA_STARTING (0x09),
  MSGMODE_FROGS_WAITING (0x33)]. That one contiguous range covers ocarina input,
  song demonstration and playback, the scarecrow recorder, the Skull Kid memory
  game and the frog choir -- every state that takes ocarina notes -- and nothing
  else. It rises when the ocarina comes out and falls when it goes away, which
  a latch never did.

ONE RESERVATION, ON THE RIGHT TRIGGER ONLY (rev3).

  A hand holding a bow/hookshot/boomerang/slingshot needs its trigger to be the
  ITEM's own N64 button as raw STATE -- press nocks, hold draws, release looses.
  rev1 reserved the trigger of whichever hand held the item, which was the OFF
  hand, which is the LEFT hand: equipping a C-item therefore took Z away and
  gave nothing back, because the mirror itself was never written. the user's
  ruling settles it: "left trigger is Z (always)". So the reservation lives on
  the SWORD hand's trigger (the right, unless left-handed), it MIRRORS
  gSohVRItemTriggerMask rather than merely dropping the binding, and the left
  trigger is Z under every condition.

  2. ORDERING AGAINST OVERLAY 0039. 0039 does its C-button work at the ONE
     merged snapshot in graph.c, at the tick boundary; this runs EARLIER, in
     padmgr, per hardware read. That order is correct and deliberate: padmgr ORs
     the Sense bits in, then graph.c masks off the C bits the RIGHT STICK
     produced. Because the Sense bits are not stick-produced, 0039's
     `~sohCFromStick` normally cannot touch them.

     KNOWN INTERACTION, recorded rather than papered over: 0039 masks by BIT,
     not by provenance. A Sense-produced C-left arriving on the same tick as a
     right-stick deflection past 25% to the left WILL be cleared by 0039, since
     the two are indistinguishable once merged. Fixing that requires 0039's mask
     to exempt Sense-produced bits; this patch does not change 0039. What it
     does do is make the collision VISIBLE instead of silent: `gSohVRSensePadBits`
     publishes exactly the mask this tick OR'd in, so a `vr` dump showing bits in
     gSohVRSensePadBits that are absent from 0039's gSohVRPadCur IS the
     collision, readable in one line.

GATING, rev5 (VR R10 verdict 5). THE MERGE RUNS IN EVERY MODE NOW.

  the user, on 1.0.1.13: "The VR controllers no longer work in 2D mode. Is there a
  way to allow it in 2D without messing up VR?" Two correct mechanisms in series
  had taken them away: overlay 0052 removed a spatial controller from SDL's
  gamepad enumeration (its right stick was playing the C buttons), and this
  merge -- the path that replaced it -- was gated on `gSohVRMode != 0`. Outside
  VR the pair was neither a gamepad nor merged, so nothing read it at all.

  The gate is now `gSohVRSenseActive != 0` alone, and `gSohVRMode` selects the
  PROFILE instead. Buttons and sticks are ordinary GameController inputs and
  need no tracking, no head pose and no ARKit session; the shell's
  SohVR_SenseFlatPump (app/ios/SohIosShell.m) is called from here, once per pad
  read, to publish them outside VR the way the compositor loop publishes them
  inside it. One producer at a time: the pump stands down while the VR loop runs.

  THE FLAT PROFILE. Classic bindings, with ONE difference, and it is the mirror
  image of the VR rule: in VR the right stick TURNS and may never be a C button;
  in 2D there is no VR turn and no item wheel, so the right stick IS the four C
  buttons, at the same 25% the LUS gamepad mapping used before 0052 removed it.
  Nothing else changes -- left stick moves, off trigger Z, sword trigger B, off
  grip R, sword grip nothing, both face buttons A and B, START on the menu
  buttons and the left thumbclick.

  The flat profile does NOT take the ocarina override. It does not need it: with
  the C buttons on the right stick the pad is an ordinary N64 controller and
  vanilla's own ocarina handling applies, exactly as it does for a gamepad.

  With no Sense pair the pad is byte-for-byte what it is today, on every target;
  the whole block is `#ifdef __IOS__`, with the vanilla call sequence preserved
  outside it, exactly as overlays 0039 and 0043 do.

rev6 (VR R16 part C) adds the ONE mode this table had left uncovered: THE VR
PAUSE MENU. the user, wearing 1.0.1.20: the left stick moves the kaleido cursor
but the right stick cannot assign an item to C-left/down/right. It could not:
0052 removed the Sense units from SDL enumeration (so LUS's stick-to-C mapping
is gone), 0039's C/turn block does not run while paused, and rev5's C gate here
was `sohVrOcarina || sohVrFlat`. The gate gains `|| sohVrPaused`, the raw
right-stick axis publish loses those same ticks so the stick never carries two
meanings, and C-UP is withheld from the stick while paused because in kaleido
C-up is a modifier, not a slot. 2D is byte-for-byte unchanged (sohVrFlat
already covered it).

rev7 (VR R18 item 2) SETTLES THE TRIGGERS WHILE AN ITEM IS OUT: BOTH OF THEM
DRIVE IT, AND NEITHER IS Z OR B.

  the user, wearing 1.0.1.22: "while holding an item, either trigger performs the
  action -- it's counterintuitive to use the RIGHT trigger when the slingshot is
  in the LEFT hand. The user hits A or B to exit, or the grip to change item."

  So the rev3 reservation stops being a reservation of ONE trigger. While the
  item in hand is one a trigger drives -- gSohVRItemTriggerBoth, published by
  overlay 0042 rev19 from Player_VrTriggerItem: the bows, the slingshot, the
  hookshot and longshot, the boomerang, the Megaton hammer and the Deku stick --
  BOTH triggers mirror gSohVRItemTriggerMask as raw STATE, and neither carries
  its classic binding:

    OFF hand   TRIGGER = the item's own N64 button   (was Z)
    SWORD hand TRIGGER = the item's own N64 button   (unchanged from rev3)

  It is a mirror of STATE on both hands, so hold-to-aim still works from either
  one, holding both is the same bit twice, and a release from either releases.

  Z-TARGETING IS THEREFORE UNAVAILABLE WHILE SUCH AN ITEM IS OUT. That is
  the user's own call, not a side effect we are living with: with the aim ray
  driving the shot there is nothing for Z to add to a bow, and the way out of an
  item is A or B (both face buttons, unchanged) or the grip's item wheel.

  NARROWER THAN THE MASK, ON PURPOSE. gSohVRItemTriggerMask is non-zero for
  EVERY non-sword C item, a bottle and a mask and the ocarina included, because
  its question is "which button does the held item live on". Widening the
  both-triggers rule to it would take Z away from a Link holding a bottle. So
  the rule reads the narrow flag, and everything not on that whitelist keeps
  rev3's behaviour exactly: sword trigger mirrors the item, off trigger is Z.

  THE OCARINA PROFILE IS UNTOUCHED, twice over: the ocarina is not on the
  whitelist, and the both-triggers branch is additionally gated on
  !sohVrOcarina, so the five notes keep the trigger bindings the profile gives
  them (D4 = A on the sword trigger, F4 = C-down on the off trigger).

The shell (app/ios/SohIosShell.m, not vendor) defines gSohVRSensePadBits,
gSohVRItemTriggerMask and gSohVRItemTriggerBoth alongside the R4 Sense globals
it already owns.

--- a/soh/src/code/padmgr.c
+++ b/soh/src/code/padmgr.c
@@ -317,7 +317,307 @@
     }
     osRecvMesg(queue, NULL, OS_MESG_BLOCK);
     osContGetReadData(padMgr->pads);
+#ifdef __IOS__
+    /* SOH_IOS (overlay 0047, VR-DONOR-MAP 8 `padmgr.c`): merge the PSVR2 Sense
+     * pair into the pad here, after the hardware read and BEFORE
+     * PadMgr_ProcessInputs, which is what derives this tick's press/rel edges.
+     * OR only -- keyboard and gamepad keep working alongside the hands. */
+    {
+        extern volatile int gSohVRMode;
+        extern volatile int gSohVRSenseActive;
+        /* rev5: reads the pair's buttons and sticks when the VR compositor loop
+         * is not running, and publishes them into the gSohVRSense* words below.
+         * A no-op while VR is live, and on targets without SohSense.m. */
+        extern void SohVR_SenseFlatPump(void);
+        extern volatile unsigned int gSohVRSenseBtn[2];
+        extern volatile float gSohVRSenseStickX[2];
+        extern volatile float gSohVRSenseStickY[2];
+        extern volatile int gSohVRSensePadBits;
+        extern volatile int gSohVRLeftHanded;
+        /* rev4: the ocarina profile's live state, and the harness override. */
+        extern volatile int gSohVROcarinaForce;
+        extern volatile int gSohVROcarinaOut;
+        /* Published by overlay 0042 on the SWORD hand; zero means "nothing
+         * reserved". rev3: it is MIRRORED as raw state, not merely reserved. */
+        extern volatile unsigned short gSohVRItemTriggerMask[2];
+        /* rev7 (VR R18 item 2): 1 while that item is one BOTH triggers drive
+         * (overlay 0042 rev19 Player_VrTriggerItem -- the bows, the slingshot,
+         * the hookshot and longshot, the boomerang, the Megaton hammer, the
+         * Deku stick). Narrower than the mask on purpose: a bottle or a mask
+         * keeps Z on the off hand. */
+        extern volatile int gSohVRItemTriggerBoth;
+        extern PlayState* gPlayState;
+        /* Donor sVrBtnMasks order: 0 TRIGGER, 1 GRIP, 2 PRIMARY, 3 SECONDARY,
+         * 4 THUMBCLICK, 5 MENU. The bit values are app/ios/SohSense.h's
+         * SOHSENSE_BTN_* -- one taxonomy shared by hardware, injection and this. */
+        static const u32 sohVrSenseMask[6] = {
+            1u << 2, 1u << 3, 1u << 0, 1u << 1, 1u << 4, 1u << 5,
+        };
+        /* rev3 (VR R8 part B): THE USER'S table, not the donor's. Row 0 = left
+         * hand, row 1 = right. Column order is sohVrSenseMask's above.
+         *   left  TRIGGER Z | GRIP R | PRIMARY(bottom) START | SECONDARY(top) A
+         *         THUMB START | MENU START
+         *   right TRIGGER B | GRIP -- (the item wheel) | PRIMARY(bottom) A
+         *         SECONDARY(top) B | THUMB C-UP (first person) | MENU START */
+        /* rev4 (VR R9 part B): the tables are BUILT, not written down, because
+         * every column that means a ROLE has to follow gSohVRLeftHanded. Row 0
+         * is the OFF hand, row 1 is the SWORD hand; the two physical columns
+         * (THUMBCLICK, which is START on the movement unit and unbound on the
+         * turn unit) are applied afterwards by physical index. */
+        static const u16 sohVrRoleClassic[2][6] = {
+            /* OFF  : TRIGGER Z | GRIP R | PRIMARY A | SECONDARY B | THUMB - | MENU START */
+            { BTN_Z, BTN_R, BTN_A, BTN_B, 0, BTN_START },
+            /* SWORD: TRIGGER B | GRIP - (item wheel) | PRIMARY A | SECONDARY B | THUMB - | MENU START */
+            { BTN_B, 0, BTN_A, BTN_B, 0, BTN_START },
+        };
+        static const u16 sohVrRoleOcarina[2][6] = {
+            { BTN_CDOWN, BTN_Z, BTN_CLEFT, BTN_CUP, 0, BTN_START },
+            { BTN_A, BTN_R, BTN_CRIGHT, BTN_B, 0, BTN_START },
+        };
+        u16 sohVrBind[2][6];
+        s32 sohVrOcarina = 0;
+        u16 sohVrBits = 0;
+        s32 sohVrHand;
+        s32 sohVrIdx;
+        s32 sohVrItemHand;
+        s32 sohVrSwordHand;
+        u16 sohVrItemMask;
+        /* rev7 (VR R18 item 2): EITHER trigger drives the held item. */
+        s32 sohVrItemBoth;
+        s32 sohVrFlat;
+        /* rev6 (VR R16 part C): the kaleido menu is open. */
+        s32 sohVrPaused;
+
+        /* rev5: OUTSIDE VR, this is what puts anything in those words at all. */
+        SohVR_SenseFlatPump();
+        sohVrFlat = (gSohVRMode == 0);
+        /* rev6 (VR R16 part C): the project's pause test, the same predicate
+         * overlays 0039/0032/0013 use -- `pauseCtx.state != 0` is every state
+         * of the kaleido menu including its open and close animations. */
+        sohVrPaused = (gPlayState != NULL) && (gPlayState->pauseCtx.state != 0);
+        if (gSohVRSenseActive != 0) {
+            /* rev3: THE OCARINA IS OUT WHEN THE MESSAGE SYSTEM SAYS SO.
+             * `msgCtx.ocarinaMode` is a LATCH -- it holds OCARINA_MODE_04 from
+             * the moment the ocarina is put away until the next textbox opens,
+             * which is frequently the rest of the session -- and reading it as
+             * "the ocarina is out" is what made the user's right bottom face
+             * button C-RIGHT and his left trigger C-DOWN long after he had
+             * finished playing. msgMode 0x09..0x33 is the contiguous range of
+             * every state that takes ocarina notes (ocarina, song demo and
+             * playback, scarecrow, memory game, frogs) and nothing else. */
+            sohVrOcarina = ((gPlayState != NULL) &&
+                            (gPlayState->msgCtx.msgMode >= MSGMODE_OCARINA_STARTING) &&
+                            (gPlayState->msgCtx.msgMode <= MSGMODE_FROGS_WAITING));
+            /* rev5 (VR R10 verdict 5): the ocarina profile is a VR profile. It
+             * exists because in VR the C buttons live on an item wheel and the
+             * notes have nowhere else to go; in 2D the right stick IS the C
+             * pad below, so the pair is an ordinary N64 controller and
+             * vanilla's own ocarina handling applies unchanged. */
+            if (sohVrFlat) {
+                sohVrOcarina = 0;
+            }
+            /* rev4: a TEST OVERRIDE, and it exists because the ocarina profile
+             * is otherwise unreachable from the harness -- reaching it for real
+             * needs an ocarina, a song and a textbox, none of which a simulator
+             * suite can arrange. -1 (the shipped value) follows the game. */
+            if (gSohVROcarinaForce >= 0) {
+                sohVrOcarina = (gSohVROcarinaForce != 0);
+            }
+            /* rev3: the reservation is the SWORD hand's trigger and no other.
+             * "left trigger is Z (always)" -- if a two-handed item needs a
+             * trigger it uses the right one. */
+            sohVrSwordHand = (gSohVRLeftHanded != 0) ? 0 : 1;
+            sohVrItemHand = sohVrSwordHand;
+            sohVrItemMask = gSohVRItemTriggerMask[sohVrItemHand];
+            /* rev7 (VR R18 item 2): the user -- "either trigger performs the
+             * action". Both hands mirror the item and neither is Z or B, for
+             * exactly the items overlay 0042 whitelists. The ocarina profile is
+             * excluded here as well as by the whitelist, because two of its five
+             * notes ARE the triggers. */
+            sohVrItemBoth = (sohVrItemMask != 0) && (gSohVRItemTriggerBoth != 0) &&
+                            !sohVrOcarina;
+            /* rev4: BUILD the physical table from the role table. */
+            for (sohVrHand = 0; sohVrHand < 2; sohVrHand++) {
+                s32 sohVrRole = (sohVrHand == sohVrSwordHand) ? 1 : 0;
+
+                for (sohVrIdx = 0; sohVrIdx < 6; sohVrIdx++) {
+                    sohVrBind[sohVrHand][sohVrIdx] =
+                        sohVrOcarina ? sohVrRoleOcarina[sohVrRole][sohVrIdx] : sohVrRoleClassic[sohVrRole][sohVrIdx];
+                }
+            }
+            /* The two PHYSICAL columns. The thumbclicks are not role-based
+             * because the STICKS are not: the left unit is always the movement
+             * stick and the right unit is always the turn. the user: "keep START
+             * on left stick click"; "right stick click: unbind". Classic only --
+             * the ocarina profile has no thumbclick binding on either hand. */
+            if (!sohVrOcarina) {
+                sohVrBind[0][4] = BTN_START;
+                sohVrBind[1][4] = 0;
+            }
+            for (sohVrHand = 0; sohVrHand < 2; sohVrHand++) {
+                u32 sohVrBtn = gSohVRSenseBtn[sohVrHand];
+
+                for (sohVrIdx = 0; sohVrIdx < 6; sohVrIdx++) {
+                    if ((sohVrIdx == 0) && (sohVrItemMask != 0) &&
+                        (sohVrItemBoth || (sohVrHand == sohVrItemHand))) {
+                        /* MIRROR, not skip. Raw STATE from the trigger onto the
+                         * item's own N64 button: press nocks, hold draws,
+                         * release looses, with every vanilla rule intact. */
+                        if (sohVrBtn & sohVrSenseMask[0]) {
+                            sohVrBits |= sohVrItemMask;
+                        }
+                        continue;
+                    }
+                    if (sohVrBtn & sohVrSenseMask[sohVrIdx]) {
+                        sohVrBits |= sohVrBind[sohVrHand][sohVrIdx];
+                    }
+                }
+            }
+            /* rev4 (VR R9 part B): THE ONLY PLACE A STICK MAY BECOME A C
+             * BUTTON. the user: "It should ONLY trigger C buttons when you're
+             * holding ocarina." Overlay 0052 removed the other route (SDL was
+             * enumerating each Sense unit as a gamepad, and SoH's default
+             * mapping binds RIGHTX/RIGHTY to the C buttons at 25%), so this is
+             * now literally the whole of it.
+             *
+             * RAW STATE, not an edge: notes are held, and PadMgr_ProcessInputs
+             * runs a few lines below and derives press/rel from cur exactly as
+             * it does for a physical button. 25% matches the threshold the
+             * removed LUS mapping used, so the deflection that used to produce
+             * a C bit still does -- in the one mode where it should. */
+            /* rev5 (VR R10 verdict 5): ...OR IN 2D, WHERE IT IS THE ONLY C
+             * PAD THERE IS. The VR rule ("the right stick turns and can never
+             * be a C button") is a rule about VR: outside it there is no smooth
+             * turn to protect and no item wheel to reach the C items through,
+             * and before overlay 0052 this same deflection produced these same
+             * bits through the LUS mapping at this same 25%. the user lost that
+             * when 0052 landed; this is where it comes back. */
+            /* rev6 (VR R16 part C): ...OR WHILE THE PAUSE MENU IS OPEN, which
+             * is the one place in VR where the C buttons are not items but
+             * SLOTS. `KaleidoScope_UpdateItemEquip`'s assignment reads
+             * `input->press.button & (BTN_CLEFT | BTN_CDOWN | BTN_CRIGHT)`
+             * (z_kaleido_item.c:696), and in VR nothing was producing those:
+             * overlay 0052 took the Sense units out of SDL's gamepad
+             * enumeration, so LUS's own right-stick-to-C mapping is gone, and
+             * 0039's C/turn block -- the other producer -- is gated on
+             * `sohVRFlat == 0`, i.e. it does not run while paused. The left
+             * stick moves the cursor (it is the N64 analog stick, published
+             * unconditionally above) and worked all along; the right stick
+             * assigned nothing because it meant nothing.
+             *
+             * The turn integrator cannot double-book the stick here: 0039's
+             * whole block is `(gSohVRMode != 0) && (sohVRFlat == 0)`, and
+             * sohVRFlat is this same pause predicate, so while paused there is
+             * no turn to protect -- and the raw axis publish below now stands
+             * down for the same ticks, so one physical stick never carries two
+             * meanings at once. */
+            if (sohVrOcarina || sohVrFlat || sohVrPaused) {
+                float sohVrOcX = gSohVRSenseStickX[1];
+                float sohVrOcY = gSohVRSenseStickY[1];
 
+                if (sohVrOcX > 0.25f) {
+                    sohVrBits |= BTN_CRIGHT;
+                } else if (sohVrOcX < -0.25f) {
+                    sohVrBits |= BTN_CLEFT;
+                }
+                if (sohVrOcY > 0.25f) {
+                    /* rev6: NO C-UP FROM THE STICK WHILE PAUSED. C-up is not an
+                     * item slot -- in kaleido it is only a modifier (the
+                     * DpadEquips chord at z_kaleido_item.c:698) and a
+                     * randomizer shortcut -- so an up-flick on the way to a
+                     * slot would toggle things rather than assign. The cursor
+                     * is on the LEFT stick, which keeps its full range. In 2D
+                     * and with the ocarina out the right stick is still the
+                     * whole C pad, C-up included. */
+                    if (sohVrOcarina || sohVrFlat) {
+                        sohVrBits |= BTN_CUP;
+                    }
+                } else if (sohVrOcY < -0.25f) {
+                    sohVrBits |= BTN_CDOWN;
+                }
+            }
+            padMgr->pads[0].button |= sohVrBits;
+            /* The LEFT stick drives the N64 analog stick, but only while it is
+             * actually deflected: writing it unconditionally would zero a
